@@ -240,3 +243,27 @@ A refusal is a `QaRootOverCapError`. Its message names the product, the total, t
 **Setting limits in code.** The variables do not always reach the test process, so pass the limits to the call: `createQaWorkspace({ appKey, rootCapBytes: 8_000_000_000, rootCeilingBytes: 0 })`, or the same options on `withQaWorkspace` (`0` disables a limit; a value that is not a non-negative number throws `TypeError`). `preflight.capBytes` and `preflight.ceilingBytes` do the same and lose to the top-level options. A value set in code wins over the variable; a limit left unset falls back to the variable, then the default. Both limits are off under vitest unless `preflight: { enabled: true }`.
 
 **The sweep removes more.** `cleanQaHomes` (run at every start) removes a home that has no `.rightkit-home.json` owner marker (written before it existed, or missing a file) when all of these hold: it is a plain directory directly inside `rightkit-qa` (never a symlink, never outside it); its name is `<label>-<6 letters or digits>` or `<label>-<16 lowercase hex>` (the two runners' patterns); no pid its records name is alive (the `ownerPid` of the run record its binding points at, and the pid of a home marker that has no binding); and nothing in it, the directory included, was modified within `RIGHTKIT_QA_HOME_MAX_AGE_SECS` (default 2 hours). Not checked: which processes have the home as working directory or an open file (there is no process-table walk), and the run record's lease (a 24 hour timer, not evidence of a live process; it is what kept cancelled runs counted). An app that holds a home and writes nothing for two hours, or a recorded pid reused by an unrelated live process, is the residual risk: the first is removed, the second is kept. Homes with an owner marker keep the earlier rule (pid dead and run older than the window).
+
+## Persistent dev root (0.6.0)
+
+`bindQaDevRoot({ app, dir })` binds one dedicated directory (for example `~/MailRightDevRoot`) as the app's persistent dev/QA home: the directory is created or adopted once (empty, or already bound to the same app; a non-empty directory without the marker is never adopted), carries `.rightkit-devroot.json`, and the launch guard (`spawnQaApp`, `assertQaLaunchSafe`, `seedFrom`) then accepts it outside system temp. Every forbidden-location rule still applies (user folders, cloud storage, volumes, UNC paths, the app's real data directory) and every directory handed to the app must stay inside the root. `disposeQaWorkspace` takes no dev root; `resetQaDevRoot(root, { data })` empties its volatile directories.
+
+## Page errors, evidence and click-until (0.6.0)
+
+`page.pageErrors()` and `page.assertNoPageErrors()` read the uncaught exceptions, unhandled rejections and `console.error` calls the page recorded; `page.captureEvidence({ directory, label })` writes the screenshot and JSON dump a timed-out wait writes; `page.clickUntil(target, until, { attempts, intervalMs })` clicks again until a page condition holds.
+
+## Control client and CLI (0.6.0)
+
+`ControlClient.connect({ suiteRoot, service })` speaks to the embedded Tauri control server of a QA build the launcher started (`RIGHTKIT_SUITE_ROOT`, `RIGHTKIT_CONTROL_SERVICE`): discovery through `service.json`, a Unix socket or named pipe (no TCP, no token), `hello` as `right-qa`, JSON-line calls (`client.call('eval', { js })`, `evaluate`, `dom`, `ax`, `screenshot`, `click`, `type`, `key`), events, chunked results. The `rightkit-qa-control` command does one call from a shell: `rightkit-qa-control eval '{"js":"return document.title"}'`. Node cannot read a socket's peer uid, so the Rust `Client` stays the stricter transport.
+
+## Visual baselines, invoke mock, control inventory (0.6.0)
+
+`compareToBaseline` and `compareDirToBaselines` (pixel diff against stored PNGs within a percent tolerance, a diff image on failure, baseline update on request; no image dependency), `createInvokeMock` and `invokeMockInstallScript` (a Tauri `invoke` mock for a browser test), `crawlControls` / `compareInventories` / `parityMarkdown` (the Rust `crawl` module's inventory and parity report in JS).
+
+## Dev-only browser preview (0.6.0)
+
+`@rightkit/qa/preview` (`selectDataSource`, `createFaultInjector`, `withFaults`, `createFixtureStore`, `loadFixtures`) is the mock data source, latency and fault injection and fixtures for a browser preview. It is not re-exported from the root, it throws at load in a production build (`NODE_ENV=production` or `import.meta.env.PROD`), and `assertNoPreviewInBundle([distDir])` fails a release build whose output contains its marker. Import it only dynamically behind `import.meta.env.DEV`.
+
+## Native dialog seam (0.6.0)
+
+`@rightkit/qa/dialog` exports `qaDialogAnswer(enabled, target?)`: the answer the harness set in `window.__RIGHTKIT_QA_DIALOG__` for the next file or folder dialog (`undefined` opens the OS dialog, `null` cancels, a path or list answers). Guard the call with a build-time QA constant so a release build drops it.
